The [[2023 Rugby World Cup]] was held in France from 8 September to 28 October 2023. Twenty teams qualified and each named an initial squad of 33 players, two more than at the previous World Cup. Squads were submitted before the tournament, and replacement players could be added during the tournament on injury grounds.<ref>{{cite news |title=When will the Rugby World Cup squads be named? |url=https://www.rugbyworld.com/tournaments/rugby-world-cup/when-will-the-rugby-world-cup-squads-be-named-154324 |website=www.rugbyworld.com |publisher=[[Rugby World]] |access-date=11 July 2023 |date=1 June 2023}}</ref>

<small>Note: The age and number of caps listed for each player is as of 8 September 2023, the first day of the tournament.</small>

==Pool A==
===New Zealand===
[[New Zealand national rugby union team|New Zealand]] announced their squad on 7 August 2023.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.allblacks.com/news/all-blacks-name-33-man-squad-for-2023-rugby-world-cup-in-france/ |title=ALL BLACKS NAME 33-MAN SQUAD FOR 2023 RUGBY WORLD CUP IN FRANCE |website=www.allblacks.com |publisher=All Blacks |date=7 August 2023 |access-date=7 August 2023}}</ref>

<sup>1</sup> On 10 September 2023, [[Emoni Narawa]] was ruled out of the World Cup, after sustaining a back injury. He was replaced in the New Zealand squad by [[Ethan Blackadder]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rugbyworldcup.com/2023/news/830571/all-blacks-name-33-man-squad-for-rugby-world-cup-2023 |title=UPDATED: All Blacks call up Blackadder to 33-man Rugby World Cup 2023 squad |publisher=Rugby World Cup |date=10 September 2023 |access-date=10 September 2023}}</ref>

